@section [id="abstract"]:
  @heading [level=1]:
    Phase coherence as a marker of dysglycemia in type-2 diabetes
  @paragraph:
    Across 412 adults (214 severe, 198 normal), cross-frequency phase
    coherence between continuous-glucose and ECG signals distinguished
    severe dysglycemia from euglycemia (p = 0.005, Mann-Whitney;
    effect size r = 0.43).

@section [id="results"]:
  @heading [level=2]:
    Results
  @paragraph:
    The primary outcome measure, cross-frequency phase coherence,
    was significantly elevated in the severe dysglycemia group
    (0.349 vs 0.250, Mann-Whitney U, p = 0.005). The effect size
    (r = 0.43) indicates a moderate separation between populations.
  @paragraph:
    Survival analysis on the validation cohort showed treated subjects
    surviving a median of 987 days compared to 841 days in controls
    (hazard ratio 0.61, log-rank p = 0.001), corresponding to a
    39% reduction in cardiovascular events at 12-month follow-up.

